DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ggeeoomm library contains the official and publicized API for interacting
     with the GEOM subsystem in the kernel.

   SSttaattiissttiiccss FFuunnccttiioonnss
     GEOM collects statistics data for all consumers and providers, but does
     not perform any normalization or presentation on the raw data, this is
     left as an exercise for user-land presentation utilities.

     The ggeeoomm__ssttaattss__ooppeenn() and ggeeoomm__ssttaattss__cclloossee() functions open and close the
     necessary pathways to access the raw statistics information in the ker‐
     nel.  These functions are likely to open one or more files and cache the
     file descriptors locally.  The ggeeoomm__ssttaattss__ooppeenn() function returns zero on
     success, and sets _e_r_r_n_o if not.

     The ggeeoomm__ssttaattss__rreessyynncc() function will check if more statistics collection
     points have been added in the kernel since ggeeoomm__ssttaattss__ooppeenn() or the pre‐
     vious call to ggeeoomm__ssttaattss__rreessyynncc().

     The ggeeoomm__ssttaattss__ssnnaappsshhoott__ggeett() function will acquire a snapshot of the raw
     data from the kernel, and while a reasonable effort is made to make this
     snapshot as atomic and consistent as possible, no guarantee is given that
     it will actually be so.  The snapshot must be freed again using the
     ggeeoomm__ssttaattss__ssnnaappsshhoott__ffrreeee() function.  The ggeeoomm__ssttaattss__ssnnaappsshhoott__ggeett() func‐
     tion returns NULL on failure.

     The ggeeoomm__ssttaattss__ssnnaappsshhoott__ttiimmeessttaammpp() function provides access to the time‐
     stamp acquired in the snapshot.

     The ggeeoomm__ssttaattss__ssnnaappsshhoott__rreesseett() and ggeeoomm__ssttaattss__ssnnaappsshhoott__nneexxtt() functions
     provide an iterator over the statistics slots in the snapshot.  The
     ggeeoomm__ssttaattss__ssnnaappsshhoott__rreesseett() function forces the internal pointer in the
     snapshot back to before the first item.  The ggeeoomm__ssttaattss__ssnnaappsshhoott__nneexxtt()
     function returns the next item, and NULL if there are no more items in
     the snapshot.

   CCoonnttrrooll FFuunnccttiioonnss
     The ggccttll__**() functions are used to send requests to GEOM classes.  In or‐
     der for a GEOM class to actually be able to receive these requests, it
     must have defined a "ctlreq" method.

     A _s_t_r_u_c_t _g_c_t_l___r_e_q _*, obtained with ggccttll__ggeett__hhaannddllee(), can hold any number
     of parameters, which must be added to it with ggccttll__rroo__ppaarraamm() (for read-
     only parameters) or ggccttll__rrww__ppaarraamm() (for read/write parameters).

     Both ggccttll__rroo__ppaarraamm() and ggccttll__rrww__ppaarraamm() take a string _n_a_m_e, which is
     used to identify the parameter, and a _v_a_l_u_e, which contains, in the read-
     only case, the data to be passed to the GEOM class, or, in the read/write
     case, a pointer to preallocated memory that the GEOM class should fill
     with the desired data.  If _l_e_n is negative, it is assumed that _v_a_l_u_e is
     an ASCII string and the actual length is taken from the string length of
     _v_a_l_u_e; otherwise it must hold the size of _v_a_l_u_e.

     A parameter with a _n_a_m_e containing the string "class" is mandatory for
     each request, and the corresponding _v_a_l_u_e must hold the name of the GEOM
     class where the request should be sent to.

     Also mandatory for each request is a parameter with a _n_a_m_e called "verb",
     and the corresponding _v_a_l_u_e needs to hold the command string that the
     GEOM class should react upon.

     Once all desired parameters are filled in, the request must be sent to
     the GEOM subsystem with ggccttll__iissssuuee(), which returns NULL on success, or a
     string containing the error message on failure.

     After the request is finished, the allocated memory should be released
     with ggccttll__ffrreeee().

     The ggccttll__dduummpp() function can be used to format the contents of _r_e_q to the
     open file handle pointed to by _f, for debugging purposes.

     Error handling for the control functions is postponed until the call to
     ggccttll__iissssuuee(), which returns NULL on success, or an error message corre‐
     sponding to the first error which happened.

   UUttiilliittyy FFuunnccttiioonnss
     The ggeeoomm__ggeettxxmmll() function is a wrapper around sysctl(3) that fetches the
     _k_e_r_n_._g_e_o_m_._c_o_n_f_x_m_l OID, and returns it's value.  The allocated memory
